WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:06.879
Batman first hit the silver screen in 1943,
and in the decades since, he's been one of

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:09.220
Hollywood's most accomplished heroes.

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:15.520
In a dozen feature films, we've seen a truly
incredible roster of Caped Crusaders and Dark

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:19.260
Knights taking the character in plenty of
different directions.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:24.100
As prominent as he’s been in film, though,
not all Batmen are created equal.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:28.200
Here's our ranking of every movie Batman,
from worst to best.

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:30.480
Batman, 1943

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:35.970
The thing you really have to understand about
the original Batman serial is that it’s

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:38.320
bad, even by the standards of the time.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:44.180
With 15 parts, it’s three and a half hours
of the worst version of Batman to ever hit

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:45.220
the silver screen.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:50.860
Batman and Robin, played by Lewis Wilson and
Douglas Croft, are recast here as government

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:56.610
agents tasked with stopping the villainous
Dr. Daka from building a death ray and creating

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:58.020
an army of zombies.

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:00.500
“I have converted him into a zombie.

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:02.820
He can only act as I direct."

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:09.520
That might sound exciting, but it’s atrocious
and repetitive, with awful special effects,

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:14.929
hilariously awkward costumes, unconvincing
fights, and let's not forget, America was

00:01:14.929 --> 00:01:20.300
at war with Japan at the time so they played
up the Japanese bad guy as you'd expect.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:23.540
This Batman is definitely best left forgotten.

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:26.340
Batman and Robin, 1949

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:32.439
The only good thing you can say about the
second Batman serial is that it's not as offensive

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:33.500
as its predecessor.

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:35.040
It’s still bad, though.

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:41.180
The bumbling, barely present Batman is trapped
in an awful plot that stretches out even longer

00:01:41.189 --> 00:01:46.560
than the first serial, with Robert Lowery's
Batman stumbling from one goofy cliffhanger

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:51.780
to the next in an attempt to unmask a mad
scientist called the Wizard.

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:53.200
After four hours, surprise!

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:58.440
He turns out to be exactly the guy you thought
he was the whole time.

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:03.860
The worst part, though, is the costume: a
straight up sweatsuit capped off with an ill-fitting

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:08.040
cowl that makes him look more like floppy-eared
bunny rabbit man.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:10.320
Batman Forever, 1995

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:17.280
In a movie that's soaked in lime green neon
and hot pink leopard print, Val Kilmer's portrayal

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:23.120
of the Caped Crusader is… well, the kindest
way to put it would be "reserved."

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:30.580
Kilmer, only two years removed from a charismatic,
scene-stealing turn as Doc Holliday in Tombstone,

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:34.840
drifts through Batman Forever with the screen
presence of a cardboard cutout.

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:39.180
To be fair, there was a good reason for Kilmer
to make that choice.

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:45.820
Director Joel Schumacher was clearly drawing
his inspiration from the 1966 television show,

00:02:45.829 --> 00:02:51.760
and with Jim Carrey and Tommy Lee Jones chewing
their way through every available bit of scenery,

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:57.820
replicating Adam West's stentorian straight
man probably seemed like the best way to go.

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:03.980
Fully interactive holograms only a high frequency
carrier wave beamed directly into the brain

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:05.340
could create such images."

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:10.939
There are parts where it works, but when it
comes to actually being Batman, his Adam West

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:12.680
impression just doesn't land.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:18.240
It’s especially rough in his tepid romance
with Nicole Kidman as Dr. Chase Meridian,

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:20.980
the worst-named love interest in the franchise.

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:23.360
"...And what a grand pursuit you must be."

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:26.800
Batman and Robin, 1997

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:32.020
To say Batman and Robin has a bad reputation
is putting it lightly.

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:37.180
It's commonly regarded as one of the worst
superhero movies of all time, with a bloated

00:03:37.189 --> 00:03:42.689
cast of villains and sidekicks sprawling out
into a plot that starts off silly and ends

00:03:42.689 --> 00:03:44.420
up full-on ludicrous.

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:50.520
It's so bad that both Joel Schumacher and
George Clooney have apologized for it, and

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:55.439
it was credited with nearly killing the franchise
until it returned with a darker direction

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:56.660
under Christopher Nolan.

00:03:57.520 --> 00:03:58.780
"Alright everyone…

00:03:59.000 --> 00:03:59.800
CHILL!

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:02.060
Chill.

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:03.600
Chill."

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:10.480
But as controversial as it might sound, Clooney
himself is not actually that bad.

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:15.680
His effortless charm doesn't really work for
Batman, but it actually does work pretty well

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:16.400
for Bruce Wayne.

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:21.580
If you can get past the toy-friendly suits
and their marble statue anatomy, Clooney's

00:04:21.580 --> 00:04:26.890
best work comes when he's out of the costume,
sweeping around Wayne Manor and dealing with

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:29.600
Alfred's not-quite-fatal illness.

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:35.200
Plus, let's be real here: even if Clooney
was as bad as the movie's reputation would

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:40.140
lead you to believe, he'd still score bonus
points for the way he refers to Dick Grayson

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:41.320
when he gets mad.

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:43.760
"She's infected us with some kind of pheromone
extract.

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:44.800
Oh is that what it is Bruce?

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:46.080
Some kind of magic spell?

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:47.580
She wants to kill you, Dick."

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:54.000
Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice and Justice
League, 2016 - 2017

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:59.800
Much of director Zack Snyder's inspiration
for Batman v. Superman came from Frank Miller's

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:03.610
landmark comic story The Dark Knight Returns.

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:08.670
Unfortunately, while the movie definitely
lifted entire scenes wholesale from the comics

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:13.520
in an effort to please hardcore fans, they're
all separated from their original context.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:19.760
Ben Affleck's Batman is a ludicrously violent
murderer who is easily manipulated into a

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:24.640
fistfight with another superhero which ends
when the person he's fighting reminds him

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:27.180
that other people also have moms.

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:30.760
"WHY DID YOU SAY THAT NAME!  It's his mother's name!"

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:36.560
Despite the movie's silly plot coincidences,
Affleck's Batman has some noteworthy fight

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:42.480
sequences, even if by this point the character
apparently gave up trying to uphold his no-kill

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:42.980
rule.

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:45.980
The LEGO Batman Movie, 2017

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:51.860
The LEGO version of Batman is the rarest kind
of satire: a parody of something that actually

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:54.740
does a great job at being the thing that it's
parodying.

00:05:55.140 --> 00:05:58.400
Will Arnett voices Batman as a full on comedy
character.

00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:03.820
He’s got an obsession with darkness, and
Arnett ramps up his naturally gravelly voice

00:06:03.820 --> 00:06:06.140
into a spoof of Christian Bale's.

00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:10.960
At the same time, though, he's very rarely
presented as anything other than extremely

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:16.280
good at what he does, an ultra-competent superhero
who really does act heroically…

00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:17.500
most of the time.

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:22.740
Either way, it works while other portrayals
might do their best to move away from the

00:06:22.750 --> 00:06:28.440
inherent silliness of a guy dressed as a bat
who primarily fights murder-clowns, embracing

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:33.490
it gives the Lego version a flavor that helps
him rival even the best of his big-screen

00:06:33.490 --> 00:06:34.380
contemporaries.

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:39.440
As funny as he might be, he's still Batman,
in one of his purest forms.

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:43.300
The Dark Knight Trilogy, 2005 - 2012

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:48.560
There’s a moment in Batman Begins where
a crooked cop gets snared by the ankle and

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:53.650
hauled up five stories in the middle of a
thunderstorm so that a vigilante who looks

00:06:53.650 --> 00:06:56.780
like Satan himself can scream in his face.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:00.320
"I don't know, I swear to God ...SWEAR TO
ME!"

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:08.840
So yeah: when you’re describing Christian
Bale’s performance, the word you want is

00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:09.540
"intense."

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:14.890
Bale's Batman brings that level of intensity
to everything he does, showing an emotional

00:07:14.890 --> 00:07:20.620
range that stretches from the righteous fury
of Batman to the aimless pain of the orphaned

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:26.150
Bruce Wayne who was denied his vengeance,
and even the quippy sarcasm of his exchanges

00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:26.720
with Alfred.

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:31.860
It's a powerful portrayal that's only ever
matched when he goes up against Heath Ledger's

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:34.820
Joker, one of the best performances in the
genre.

00:07:35.540 --> 00:07:39.660
Batman/Batman Returns, 1989 - 1992

00:07:40.180 --> 00:07:45.300
Here's the thing about Tim Burton's Batman
movies: they're great Tim Burton movies.

00:07:45.580 --> 00:07:49.660
They have an incredible visual style, especially
when it comes to Gotham City.

00:07:49.670 --> 00:07:55.520
It’s a hellish industrial urban sprawl slammed
against retro Art Deco, where everyone still

00:07:55.520 --> 00:08:01.960
dresses like it's 1940 and where purple knockout
gas is a deadly threat against a monochromatic

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:02.460
cityscape.

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:08.120
They're full of great bits of satire, beautiful
cinematography, and the casting is amazingly

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:14.060
offbeat, pitting our hero against Jack Nicholson,
Michelle Pfeiffer, Jack Palance, and Christopher

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:14.560
Walken.

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:20.020
The problem is that DC and Warner Brothers
were still trying to figure out what Batman

00:08:20.020 --> 00:08:22.820
and Bruce Wayne should be like in the modern
era.

00:08:23.420 --> 00:08:29.030
The result was some awkward exchanges between
Bruce Wayne and Vicki Vale, as we see Batman's

00:08:29.030 --> 00:08:30.260
human side struggle.

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:35.070
No one really captures that disconnect more
than Michael Keaton did when he put on the

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:36.160
cape and cowl.

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:41.120
He’s actually kind of perfect for the kind
of remote, mysterious weirdo who sleeps upside

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:43.540
down because he wants to be more like a bat.

00:08:43.820 --> 00:08:44.960
“You wanna get nuts?

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:45.820
Come on!

00:08:46.220 --> 00:08:46.880
Let’s get nuts!”

00:08:47.140 --> 00:08:51.700
But there’s a difference between the stylized
goofiness that Burton explored in movies like

00:08:51.710 --> 00:08:56.900
Pee Wee's Big Adventure and Beetlejuice, and
the '80s action movie aesthetic that saw Keaton's

00:08:56.900 --> 00:08:59.600
Batman blowing up buildings full of criminals.

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:04.940
Batman’s actions are mostly portrayed as
a personal quest for vengeance that only incidentally

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:07.680
winds up saving a city, and that’s a shame.

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:13.510
Bruce Wayne's borderline psycho exchanges
aside, Michael Keaton ultimately nails the

00:09:13.510 --> 00:09:19.100
dark, almost spectral version of Batman that
terrifies most of Gotham's criminals.

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:21.240
"I want you to tell all your friends about
me.

00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:22.160
What are you?

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:24.740
I’m Batman."

00:09:25.820 --> 00:09:28.680
Batman: The Movie, 1966

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:34.500
The best thing about Adam West's performance
in Batman '66 is that it can mean completely

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:36.460
different things depending when you watch
it.

00:09:36.700 --> 00:09:42.260
As a child, it's easy to buy West’s Batman
as completely genuine, and as an adult, once

00:09:42.260 --> 00:09:46.580
you're in on the joke, the fact that he was
able to play the perfect straight man for

00:09:46.580 --> 00:09:52.620
a wild cast of villains is every bit as astonishing
as those surface-level comic book thrills.

00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:58.460
The movie, released on the big screen in 1966
between the first two seasons of the show,

00:09:58.460 --> 00:10:00.860
has everything, in a very literal sense.

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:05.840
The arch-villains this time around are four
of his greatest foes brought together as the

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:11.620
United Underworld, revealed with a sequence
in which Batman and Robin identify their enemies

00:10:11.620 --> 00:10:14.460
through profound leaps of logic.

00:10:14.900 --> 00:10:16.720
“You mean where there's a fish there could
be a Penguin.

00:10:16.980 --> 00:10:17.500
But wait!

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:18.740
It happened at sea… see?

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:21.820
C for Catwoman!”

00:10:22.580 --> 00:10:27.660
At the same time, it’s easy to forget that
West is actually really good as Batman, particularly

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:30.540
when he goes undercover as Bruce Wayne to
save his love interest, Miss Kitka.

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:34.740
The lengths he’s willing to go, and the
heartbreak in his eyes when he discovers that

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:36.710
she was Catwoman all along?

00:10:36.710 --> 00:10:40.600
Even if you're watching it with a healthy
level of irony, it's a powerful moment.

00:10:40.950 --> 00:10:46.580
No actor has ever been as strongly identified
with the role of Batman as Adam West, and

00:10:46.580 --> 00:10:48.700
this movie shows exactly why.

00:10:48.890 --> 00:10:53.600
His performance walks a delicate balance between
full-hearted adventure and witty satire, and

00:10:53.600 --> 00:10:55.080
the end result is perfect.

00:10:55.500 --> 00:11:01.630
He is and always will be the Batman who won't
even risk hurting a bunch of baby ducks.

00:11:02.780 --> 00:11:04.900
“Some days you just can’t get rid of a
bomb!”

00:11:06.100 --> 00:11:09.040
Batman: Mask of the Phantasm, 1993

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:12.320
Okay, we'll admit that this one is a bit of
a cheat.

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:19.670
Kevin Conroy has, after all, been the most
prominent Batman actor for more than 25 years,

00:11:19.670 --> 00:11:25.070
making his debut on Batman: The Animated Series
and staying in the role all the way through

00:11:25.070 --> 00:11:26.740
the Arkham video games.

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:31.680
But there's a good reason for that: Conroy
is an amazing Batman, and everything that

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:34.780
makes him great is on display in Mask of the
Phantasm.

00:11:35.240 --> 00:11:40.460
The film is the first time that the Animated
Series really dug into Batman’s origin story,

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:43.140
and Conroy's performance sold it beautifully.

00:11:43.520 --> 00:11:47.930
The scene when he's pleading with his parents'
graves, telling them he doesn’t want to

00:11:47.930 --> 00:11:52.680
fight crime because he's finally found happiness
is devastatingly emotional.

00:11:54.760 --> 00:11:58.280
"I know I made a promise, but I didn't see
this coming.

00:11:59.400 --> 00:12:00.760
I didn’t count on being happy"

00:12:00.820 --> 00:12:06.380
Even better is when that happiness is taken
away and Bruce gives himself over to his mission

00:12:06.380 --> 00:12:07.020
entirely.

00:12:07.720 --> 00:12:12.380
For a generation of Batman fans, Conroy's
is the voice they hear in their heads when

00:12:12.380 --> 00:12:17.200
they read comics, and that alone should show
just how great he was in the role.

00:12:17.680 --> 00:12:21.660
It takes an incredible amount of talent to
redefine the character

00:12:21.660 --> 00:12:26.620
but that’s exactly what Kevin
Conroy did, in a way that no other Batman

00:12:26.620 --> 00:12:27.029
could.

